<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>More Fun Problems! (Chapters 2 and 3)</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Remember these facts about congruence arithmetic!
            If
            <md>
                a\equiv b \pmod{m}
            </md>
            and
            <md>
                c\equiv d \pmod{m}
            </md>,
            then
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>a+c \equiv b+d \pmod{m}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>a-c \equiv b-d \pmod{m}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>ac \equiv bd \pmod{m}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>a^n \equiv b^n \pmod{m}</m> for any
                        <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <activity>
        <title>Practice Proofs Using Mod and Divisibility</title>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Fill in the blank and prove.
                    If <m>n\equiv 2 \pmod{7}</m> then
                    <m>n^2+n+2 \equiv <fillin characters="3"/>\pmod{7} </m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   If <m>n\equiv 5 \pmod{8}</m> then <m>8 \mid n^2 -1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Roster Notation to Set Builder Notation Practice</title>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The integers that leave remainder 3 when divided by 7.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The positive divisors of 18.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The multiples of 5 that are between 1 and 50.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\{\dots, -81, 9, 99, 189, 279, 369,\dots\}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\{1,3,9,27,81, \dots\}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What are the possible remainders when you square an integer
                    and divide by 8?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    When you square a natural number and divide by 6, show that
                    you always get a remainder of 0, 1, or 3.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Recall the fact we briefly explored: if <m>p</m> is a prime
                number greater than <m>3</m> then <m> 24 \mid p^2 - 1 </m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's start by showing <m>3\mid p^2 -1</m> for any prime
                    greater than <m>3</m>.
                    Hint: Use cases, and the congruence algebra at the start.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Can you do a similar thing to show that <m>8\mid p^2 -1</m>?
                    Since <m>8</m> and <m>3</m> are relatively prime (have no
                    factors in common), this will complete the proof that
                    <m>24 \mid p^2-1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the remainder when you divide <m>3^{100}</m> by
                    <m>2</m>?
                    Said differently, find <m>3^{100} \pmod{2}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the remainder when you divide an <m>2^{100}</m> by
                    <m>3</m>?
                    Said differently, find <m>2^{100} \pmod{3}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the remainder when you divide an <m>3^{2026}</m> by
                    <m>7</m>?
                    Said differently, find <m>3^{2026} \pmod{7}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                You may have seen the trick in which you know an integer is
                divisible by <m>3</m> if the sum of its digits is divisible by
                <m>3</m>.
                E.g., we know <m>123</m> is divisible by <m>3</m> because the
                sum of its digits, <m>1+2+3=6</m>, is divisible by <m>3</m>.
                Let's prove that!
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We can write out <m>123</m> the place value way, i.e.,
                    <md>
                        123 = 1\cdot 100 + 2\cdot 10 + 3\cdot 1
                    </md>.
                    Pick another number, say a 4 digit one and write it out the
                    place value way too.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Remember that trick where we replaced <m>9</m> with
                    <m>8 + 1</m>?
                    Can you do some similar algebra with <m>123</m> and see how
                    the sum of its digits comes into play.
                    (Hint: Don't forget you are thinking about divisibility by
                    <m>3</m>!)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Try the same thing for the number you used in the first
                    task.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Can you generalize this idea to any number?
                    That is, show that for any integer, the sum of its digits is
                    divisible by <m>3</m> if and only if the number itself is
                    divisible by <m>3</m>.
                    Consider a number written out by it's digits, i.e.,
                    <m>d_kd_{k-1}d_{k-2}\dots d_1d_0</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Say the sum of the digits has remainder <m>1</m> or <m>2</m>
                    when divided by <m>3</m>.
                    What can you conclude about the remainder when the number
                    itself is divided by <m>3</m>?
                    In other words, what is <m>n \pmod{3}</m> for any integer
                    <m>n</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does the same trick work with negative numbers?
                    How?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    There are lots of tricks like this.
                    You can do these tricks with different divisors, and also in
                    different number bases as well (like binary).
                    Feel free to make a conjecture and use as your play,
                    conjecture, prove!
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>